<p>Ukraine said it has asked Turkey, along with other capitals, about hosting a meeting between President Volodymyr Zelenskyy and Russian President Vladimir Putin as Kyiv tries to revive peace efforts. Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha disclosed the request in comments to reporters on Tuesday that were cleared for release on Wednesday, a sign that Kyiv is still pushing for direct, top-level diplomacy. ABC described the proposed summit as a possible way to break an impasse in U.S.-led peace efforts. The move also fits Zelenskyy&#x27;s long-running push for direct talks with Putin as a way to speed progress toward ending the war.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Cr9uKYuH8lVRy4SLadYp7yrgZYClR74xhx9_BnZOwOeix8dPIcarLuGInMuq2Xi7xv58CI65VLPGJajrm9SuT8lym983ZtlWWSr1mHVVQtJsRHI3S8-KVNCEZvh6rWQgidVIqfg5o1re3qlKM3qkKgMdPTxiIdThcK7HXbFb2A' alt='Image accompanying ABC News coverage of Ukraine&#x27;s push for a Zelenskyy-Putin summit.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Other venues: Sybiha said Kyiv asked Turkey and also contacted other capitals about hosting, suggesting Ukraine is keeping several diplomatic channels open at once.</li><li>Public signal: The minister made the remarks to reporters on Tuesday, and they were cleared for release on Wednesday, pointing to a deliberate public push behind the summit proposal.</li><li>US-led track: ABC described the proposed leaders&#x27; meeting as an effort to jolt U.S.-led peace talks that had stalled, giving the summit idea a broader diplomatic purpose.</li></ul><blockquote>We asked the Turks about it, we asked some other capitals, - Andrii Sybiha</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Ukraine&#x27;s foreign ministry: Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha publicly backed a Turkey-hosted Zelenskyy-Putin meeting as part of Kyiv&#x27;s effort to restart diplomacy. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/ukraine-has-asked-turkey-host-zelenskiy-putin-meeting-fm-says'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Ukraine&#x27;s leadership: Kyiv 